    "case_id": "523",
    "case_summary": "A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis secondary to alcohol use disorder in remission. Past medical history included esophageal varices, rectal varices, ascites, spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, and recurrent pleural effusion. Risk factors included a remote history of intranasal cocaine use and multiple tattoos. No history of blood transfusions before 1990, intravenous drug use, or high-risk sexual behavior. Three years after initial evaluation, she developed clinical decompensation requiring multiple paracenteses, thoracenteses, and blood transfusions for gastrointestinal bleeding. Three months into this period of decline, she had a dental extraction.\n\nPatient presented with hepatic encephalopathy, acute kidney injury, and recurrent pleural effusion. Mental status showed acute decline. Progressive deterioration led to increased encephalopathy with concurrent hypotension requiring critical care unit transfer.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  * Aspartate aminotransferase and alanine aminotransferase elevated to greater than 1000 U/L\n  * Negative urine drug screen\n  * Negative blood cultures\n  * Initial negative HCV antibody, later positive\n  * HCV viral load: 4,510,319 copies/mL (Hospital Day 4)\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  * Doppler ultrasound scan of liver vasculature performed\n\n- Other Tests:\n  * Negative evaluation for Epstein-Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus\n  * Negative thoracentesis and paracentesis\n\n- Pathology:\n  Title: Liver Explant Histology\n  Description: Demonstrated micronodular cirrhosis with dense fibrosis, scattered simple cysts, and bile duct hamartomas",
